# Migrate Flat Pulumi Program to `ComponentResource` Without Replacement

Moving resources into a `ComponentResource` changes their Pulumi URN path (e.g., `index/bucket` → `index/Spine/bucket`). By default, Pulumi treats this as a deletion + recreation. **Aliases bridge the old and new URNs**, instructing Pulumi to preserve state and apply only structural/config updates.

## 1. Extract Legacy URNs (One-Time)
Run this in your current flat program stack to capture exact URNs:
```bash
pulumi state list --json | jq -r '.[] | "\(.type)\t\(.urn)"' > legacy-urns.tsv
```
You'll see lines like:
```
aws:s3/bucket:Bucket	urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::index/assets-bucket
aws:rds/instance:Instance	urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::aws:rds/instance:Instance::index/app-db
```

## 2. ComponentResource Implementation (`src/Spine.ts`)
```typescript
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as eks from "@pulumi/eks";

export interface SpineArgs {
    env: "dev" | "prod";
    region: string;
    // Map logical resource names -> exact legacy URNs
    legacyUrnMap: Record<string, string>;
}

export class Spine extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
    readonly cluster: eks.Cluster;
    readonly bucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
    readonly db: aws.rds.Instance;
    readonly role: aws.iam.Role;
    readonly dbSecret: pulumi.Output<string>;

    constructor(name: string, args: SpineArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
        super("infra:Spine", name, {}, opts);

        const isProd = args.env === "prod";
        const config = new pulumi.Config();
        const dbPassword = config.requireSecret("dbPassword"); // ✅ AC: Secret never plaintext

        // Helper: inject alias if legacy URN exists
        const alias = (key: string): pulumi.CustomResourceOptions => {
            const urn = args.legacyUrnMap[key];
            return urn ? { aliases: [{ urn }] } : {};
        };

        // 1. Networking (simplified for focus)
        const vpc = new aws.ec2.Vpc(`${name}-vpc`, { cidrBlock: "10.0.0.0/16" }, alias("vpc"));
        const subnetGroup = new aws.rds.SubnetGroup(`${name}-subnets`, {
            subnetIds: vpc.publicSubnetIds,
        }, alias("db-subnet-group"));

        // 2. EKS Cluster ✅ AC: Managed node groups, env-sizing
        this.cluster = new eks.Cluster(`${name}-eks`, {
            vpcId: vpc.id,
            subnetIds: vpc.publicSubnetIds,
            instanceType: isProd ? "t3.large" : "t3.medium",
            desiredCapacity: isProd ? 4 : 2,
            minSize: isProd ? 4 : 2,
            maxSize: isProd ? 6 : 4,
            managedNodeGroups: [
                {
                    name: `${name}-workers`,
                    instanceTypes: [isProd ? "t3.large" : "t3.medium"],
                    minSize: isProd ? 4 : 2,
                    maxSize: isProd ? 6 : 4,
                    desiredCapacity: isProd ? 4 : 2,
                }
            ],
        }, alias("eks-cluster"));

        // 3. S3 Bucket ✅ AC: Versioning, AES-256, no public access
        this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket(`${name}-assets`, {
            versioning: { enabled: true },
            serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
                rules: [{ applyServerSideEncryptionConfiguration: { sseAlgorithm: "AES256" } }]
            },
            publicAccessBlock: {
                blockPublicAcls: true,
                blockPublicPolicy: true,
                ignorePublicAcls: true,
                restrictPublicBuckets: true,
            },
        }, alias("s3-assets"));

        // Prod-only cross-region replication (omitted for brevity; follows same alias pattern)
        if (isProd) {
            new aws.s3.BucketReplicationConfiguration(`${name}-repl-config`, {
                bucket: this.bucket.id,
                role: "<replication-role-arn>",
                rules: [{
                    status: "Enabled",
                    destination: { bucket: "<replica-bucket-arn>", region: "us-west-2" },
                }],
            }, alias("s3-repl-config"));
        }

        // 4. RDS PostgreSQL ✅ AC: Deletion protection, 7d backup, private, multi-AZ (prod)
        this.db = new aws.rds.Instance(`${name}-db`, {
            engine: "postgres",
            engineVersion: "15.4",
            instanceClass: isProd ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
            allocatedStorage: 20,
            publiclyAccessible: false,
            deletionProtection: true,
            backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
            multiAz: isProd,
            storageEncrypted: isProd,
            kmsKeyId: isProd ? "<prod-kms-key-arn>" : undefined,
            dbName: "appdb",
            username: "appuser",
            password: dbPassword,
            vpcSecurityGroupIds: [vpc.defaultSecurityGroup.id],
            dbSubnetGroupName: subnetGroup.name,
        }, alias("rds-db"));

        // 5. IAM Role & Policy ✅ AC: Least privilege, no wildcards on prod
        this.role = new aws.iam.Role(`${name}-svc-role`, {
            assumeRolePolicy: isProd
                ? JSON.stringify({
                    Version: "2012-10-17",
                    Statement: [{
                        Effect: "Allow",
                        Principal: { Federated: `arn:aws:iam::${pulumi.getStack()}:oidc-provider/eks.oidc.${args.region}.amazonaws.com/...` },
                        Action: "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
                        Condition: { StringEquals: { "eks.oidc...:sub": "system:serviceaccount:default:webapp" } }
                    }]
                })
                : JSON.stringify({
                    Version: "2012-10-17",
                    Statement: [{ Effect: "Allow", Principal: { AWS: "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:root" }, Action: "sts:AssumeRole" }]
                }),
        }, alias("iam-role"));

        new aws.iam.RolePolicy(`${name}-svc-policy`, {
            role: this.role,
            policy: isProd
                ? JSON.stringify({
                    Version: "2012-10-17",
                    Statement: [{
                        Effect: "Allow",
                        Action: ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject", "rds-db:Connect"],
                        Resource: [this.bucket.arn.apply(a => `${a}/*`), this.db.arn]
                    }]
                })
                : JSON.stringify({
                    Version: "2012-10-17",
                    Statement: [{ Effect: "Allow", Action: ["s3:*", "rds:*"], Resource: "*" }] // Dev programmatic access
                }),
        }, alias("iam-policy"));

        // 6. HTTPS Exposure ✅ AC: Listener on 443, valid cert, no forced redirect
        const alb = new aws.lb.LoadBalancer(`${name}-alb`, {
            internal: !isProd, // Dev: internal ALB | Prod: internet-facing
            loadBalancerType: "application",
            subnets: vpc.publicSubnetIds,
        }, alias("alb"));

        new aws.lb.Listener(`${name}-https-listener`, {
            loadBalancerArn: alb.arn,
            port: 443,
            protocol: "HTTPS",
            sslPolicy: "ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06",
            certificateArn: isProd ? "<acm-cert-arn>" : "<dev-cert-arn>",
            defaultActions: [{ type: "forward", targetGroupArn: "<tg-arn>" }], // No default 80→443 redirect
        }, alias("alb-listener"));

        // 7. Secret Construction ✅ AC: Encrypted at rest via ConfigSecret
        this.dbSecret = pulumi.interpolate`postgresql://${this.db.username}:${dbPassword}@${this.db.endpoint}/appdb`;

        this.registerOutputs({
            clusterEndpoint: this.cluster.endpoint,
            bucketArn: this.bucket.arn,
            dbEndpoint: this.db.endpoint,
            secret: this.dbSecret,
        });
    }
}
```

## 3. Stack Entry & Configs (`index.ts`, `Pulumi.*.yaml`)

**`index.ts`**
```typescript
import { Spine } from "./Spine";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

const config = new pulumi.Config();
const env = config.get("env") as "dev" | "prod" || "dev";
const region = config.get("region") || (env === "prod" ? "us-west-2" : "us-east-1");

// Paste exact URNs extracted in Step 1
const legacyUrnMap: Record<string, string> = {
    "vpc": "urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::aws:ec2/vpc:Vpc::index/vpc",
    "eks-cluster": "urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::eks:cluster:Cluster::index/eks",
    "s3-assets": "urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::index/assets",
    "rds-db": "urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::aws:rds/instance:Instance::index/db",
    "iam-role": "urn:pulumi:dev::webapp::aws:iam/role:Role::index/role",
    // ... add all resources
};

const spine = new Spine("webapp-spine", { env, region, legacyUrnMap });

export const outputs = {
    clusterEndpoint: spine.clusterEndpoint,
    bucketArn: spine.bucketArn,
    dbSecret: spine.secret,
};
```

**`Pulumi.dev.yaml` / `Pulumi.prod.yaml`**
```yaml
env: dev
region: us-east-1
dbPassword:
  secret: true
# ... other stack-native secrets
```

## 4. Safe Migration Execution

1. **Preview First** (Critical):
   ```bash
   pulumi preview --diff
   ```
   ✅ **Expected**: `~ update` or `no changes` for all resources. Zero `replacing` or `deleting`.
   ❌ **If replacements appear**: Verify `legacyUrnMap` matches exact URNs from `pulumi state list`.

2. **Apply**:
   ```bash
   pulumi up --yes
   ```

3. **Cleanup**: After successful deployment, remove `legacyUrnMap` from `index
